{
  /* ==============================================================================================
   * The TypeScript compiler contract for the extracted Slatwall Catalog slice.
   *
   * This is the DEVELOPMENT / TYPE-CHECK program. It type-checks src/** and test/**, so the test
   * tree is held to production strictness rather than to a relaxed test-only configuration. The
   * sibling tsconfig.build.json extends this file and narrows the program to shippable src/** for
   * the packaging step, so nothing test-only can reach a Lambda artifact. eslint.config.mjs uses
   * typescript-eslint with `projectService: true`, so the "include" list below is also the file set
   * the type-aware lint rules operate on.
   *
   * Nothing outside this subtree is ever part of a program defined here: the CFML tree is
   * reference-only and is not compiled.
   *
   * These settings are a contract, not a starting point. When a source file fails to compile, THE
   * SOURCE FILE IS FIXED; this configuration is never weakened to let code through.
   * ============================================================================================ */
  "compilerOptions": {
    /* --- Language and runtime target ----------------------------------------------------------
     * ES2022 is the language level of the Node 20 Lambda runtime this service targets, and the
     * level build/esbuild.mjs emits for (--target=node20). Keeping the two in step means a
     * construct that type-checks is a construct the runtime can execute.
     *
     * ⚠️ RUNTIME LIFECYCLE DISCLOSURE — THE NODE 20 TARGET IS KNOWN TO BE OUT OF SUPPORT, AND
     * THE PIN STANDS ANYWAY. This is a deliberate, cited decision, not an oversight, and it is
     * recorded here as well as in the plan so that a reader of the configuration finds it without
     * having to read the plan first. The verified dates:
     *   - Node.js 20 reached upstream end-of-life on 2026-03-24.
     *   - AWS deprecated the Lambda `nodejs20.x` managed runtime on 2026-04-30; after that date
     *     AWS stops applying security patches and removes the runtime from the console's creation
     *     list, though it stays selectable through the CLI, CloudFormation, SAM and CDK, and
     *     already-deployed functions remain invocable.
     *   - Published AWS timelines CONFLICT on the subsequent hard gates. One gives a block on new
     *     function creation at 2026-08-31 and on function updates at 2026-09-30; another gives
     *     2027-02-01 and 2027-03-03. Both are recorded because neither can be treated as settled.
     *
     * WHY NOTHING HERE CHANGES. Node 20.x / `nodejs20.x` is the runtime the frozen plan specifies,
     * and AAP 0.5.5 resolves the conflict explicitly — "The pin stands." — while AAP 0.5.3.2
     * deliberately REJECTS the newer @types/node major for the same reason. Retargeting the
     * runtime is a product decision recorded against the plan, not something this configuration
     * may take unilaterally: aligning the code to the plan is the instruction, and silently
     * contradicting the frozen runtime target would be the worse failure. The disclosure is the
     * deliverable here; the migration is not.
     *
     * THE BLAST RADIUS IS SMALL BY CONSTRUCTION, AND A LATER MOVE IS MECHANICAL. Infrastructure as
     * code is out of scope, so no `Runtime: nodejs20.x` string is authored anywhere in this
     * subtree. Exactly four artifacts are version-coupled — `--target=node20` in
     * build/esbuild.mjs, the `engines` field together with .nvmrc, the @types/node pin, and the
     * version statements in the subtree README — and the hexagonal boundary confines every AWS
     * type to src/handlers/**, so a runtime bump touches those four plus this "target"/"lib" pair
     * and nothing under src/domain/**, src/ports/**, src/services/** or src/adapters/**. None of
     * the above is a performance, latency, throughput or availability claim; no such figure is
     * stated anywhere in this port (AAP IR-12).
     *
     * "lib" is pinned to ES2022 alone, so the browser (window / document / fetch-era) typings
     * TypeScript would otherwise make ambiently available are deliberately absent. This is a
     * headless service — Lambda handlers returning data, with no rendering layer (AAP 0.3.4). The
     * one in-scope legacy file carrying a view extension,
     * integrationServices/google/views/feed/product.cfm, emits RSS 2.0 XML for a merchant feed
     * processor, so its port ProductFeedBuilder.ts is a serializer and not a component. With the
     * browser typings withheld, an accidental reference to a browser-only global is a compile
     * error here instead of a failure inside a Lambda invocation. */
    "target": "ES2022",
    "lib": ["ES2022"],

    /* --- Module system -----------------------------------------------------------------------
     * NodeNext for both "module" and "moduleResolution". Under NodeNext the nearest package.json
     * "type" field decides each file's module format, and package.json declares "type": "commonjs",
     * so every .ts file resolves and emits as CommonJS — matching the --format=cjs bundle the Node
     * 20 Lambda runtime loads and ts-jest's default. tsc, ts-jest and esbuild therefore agree on
     * module semantics with no runtime resolver shim.
     *
     * NO MODULE PATH ALIASES ARE DECLARED — neither `baseUrl` nor `paths`. Every intra-subtree
     * import is relative. This is a correctness requirement: esbuild does not honour tsconfig alias
     * maps, so an alias that type-checks can fail to resolve at bundle time and the packaged
     * handler would throw MODULE_NOT_FOUND on cold start. Identical resolution across both tools
     * also keeps the hexagonal boundary visible in the import line itself. Consequently
     * jest.config.ts needs no moduleNameMapper alias block. */
    "module": "NodeNext",
    "moduleResolution": "NodeNext",

    /* --- Strictness: MANDATED, AND NEVER TO BE WEAKENED ---------------------------------------
     * AAP 0.7.3 standard 1 ("strict type safety") is declared by exactly these three flags, and
     * this file is its enforcement point. No relaxing option may ever be added here — not
     * `noImplicitAny: false`, not `strictNullChecks: false`, not a suppression flag — and a
     * failing compile is always resolved in the offending source file.
     *
     * Two of them have consequences a reader should expect rather than be surprised by, because
     * surfacing these cases is the point of choosing the flags:
     *
     *   noUncheckedIndexedAccess types every indexed read as `T | undefined`. That is directly
     *   load-bearing for carried defect D13: model/service/SkuService.cfc:L223-L269 indexes
     *   sortedArrayReturn[index] where index = arrayFind(...) can legitimately be 0 because
     *   getSortedProductSkusID returns only option-bearing SKUs, and the CFML assignment then
     *   throws. Under this flag the hazard is visible at compile time. Per AAP 0.7.3 standard 7
     *   ("preserve and annotate, do not repair") the ported behaviour is still CARRIED and marked
     *   with the plan's parity annotation at the call site — this flag documents the defect, it
     *   does not license fixing it.
     *
     *   exactOptionalPropertyTypes distinguishes "property absent" from "property present and
     *   undefined". That distinction is what lets the option-to-SKU semantics of AAP 0.6.1.3 be
     *   expressed in types: T2 (productID is unconditionally supplied on the only real call path,
     *   so it is typed REQUIRED rather than optional) and T5 (an empty selectedOptions list is a
     *   legal, meaningful input that degenerates to "all option-bearing SKUs of this product" and
     *   must not be guarded away). */
    "strict": true,
    "exactOptionalPropertyTypes": true,
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true,

    /* --- Additional strictness (compatible with, and never contradicting, the mandated three) --
     * noImplicitOverride matters for this port specifically: the legacy tree reuses behaviour by
     * template-method inheritance and `super.save()`, whereas the target composes an injected
     * BaseService (AAP 0.4.3.3). Requiring an explicit `override` keyword keeps any remaining
     * subclassing honest. noImplicitReturns and noFallthroughCasesInSwitch guard the branch-heavy
     * translations — most notably the three-way baseProductType discriminator in
     * SkuService.createSkus, whose fallthrough must throw rather than silently return.
     * useUnknownInCatchVariables is implied by "strict" and is stated explicitly because the port
     * catches and re-wraps errors through DomainError, which requires narrowing from unknown. */
    "noImplicitOverride": true,
    "noImplicitReturns": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "useUnknownInCatchVariables": true,

    /* --- Interop and hygiene ------------------------------------------------------------------
     * isolatedModules keeps tsc's semantics aligned with the per-file transpilation performed by
     * esbuild and ts-jest, so a construct that needs whole-program information is rejected here
     * rather than misbehaving in the bundle. esModuleInterop lets the CommonJS runtime dependency
     * be imported with standard ESM syntax.
     *
     * isolatedModules also makes ts-jest transform each test file individually instead of building
     * a whole program, so the runner reports runtime failures but not type errors. Type checking of
     * the test tree therefore belongs to the typecheck command, which is why "test/**" appears in
     * this file's "include" list.
     *
     * skipLibCheck excludes dependency .d.ts files, keeping the signal on this subtree's own
     * source. It is safe because the type packages are exact-pinned rather than floating, and
     * @types/node is deliberately held on the 20.x line so it describes the APIs the Node 20
     * runtime actually has.
     *
     * "types" is deliberately NOT declared. Automatic @types inclusion covers node, jest and
     * aws-lambda together; naming a subset would silently drop the aws-lambda definitions from the
     * handler layer, the one place AWS coupling is permitted. */
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,

    /* --- Emit --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     * This program never emits: it exists to type-check. tsconfig.build.json is the only
     * configuration that flips "noEmit" to false, which is why declaring it true here is what
     * makes that override meaningful and stops a bare `tsc -p tsconfig.json` from scattering
     * JavaScript into dist/. ts-jest is unaffected because it forces noEmit false internally for
     * its own in-memory transform.
     *
     * The rootDir / outDir / sourceMap / declaration quartet describes the emit layout that
     * tsconfig.build.json inherits and narrows (rootDir "src"). Declaration files are not
     * produced: nothing outside this subtree consumes it as a typed library — the deliverable is
     * bundled Lambda artifacts. No "composite" or project-references setup is declared either,
     * since that would change the emit topology for no benefit at this size.
     *
     * Two option families are absent on purpose and must stay absent. Decorator metadata options
     * are not declared, because AAP 0.7.3 standard 3 requires explicit constructor injection with
     * no service locator, no dynamic method synthesis and no string-keyed runtime resolution —
     * decorator-driven containers are exactly the runtime metaprogramming this port exists to
     * retire (org/Hibachi/HibachiService.cfc:L255-L281 fabricated the whole implicit CRUD surface
     * via onMissingMethod; IR-1 requires those members to be declared instead). And JavaScript
     * sources are not admitted to the program at all: the subtree's two .mjs files, the lint
     * configuration and the packaging script, are deliberately outside every TypeScript program. */
    "noEmit": true,
    "rootDir": ".",
    "outDir": "dist",
    "sourceMap": true,
    "declaration": false
  },

  /* Program scope. src/** and test/** are both checked so the test suite is held to production
   * strictness, and jest.config.ts is listed so the runner's own configuration is type-checked too.
   * build/esbuild.mjs is intentionally absent: it is .mjs and belongs to no TypeScript program.
   * Every entry is subtree-relative, so the program cannot reach the CFML tree. */
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts", "test/**/*.ts", "jest.config.ts"],
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "dist", "coverage"]
}
